*On your server:**\n\n1. Run `setup-git-server.sh` once to initialize:\n   - Bare git repository at `/git/server`\n   - Post-receive hook that auto-deploys on push\n   - Web directory at `/var/www/html`\n\n2. Start the web server with `start-webserver.sh` (keep it running)\n\n**On your local machine:**\n\nFollow the workflow in `client-workflow.md`:\n- Clone from `user@server:/git/server`\n- Make changes, commit, and push\n- Files automatically deploy to `/var/www/html`\n- Access via `curl http://server:8080/hello.html`\n\nThe post-receive hook is the key—it checks out your repository files to `/var/www/html` whenever you push, so HTTP requests get the latest code immediately. I've also included an optional nginx config if you want more robust serving instead of Python's simple server.","ttft_ms":4845,"type":"result","duration_ms":23675,"uuid":"66d72077-4488-4582-82cf-24f0d814d57d","ttft_stream_ms":1056,"time_to_request_ms":81,"queued_turn_count":0}
